    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Concept Overview</h2>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 1.5rem;">
            <li><strong>Linear Search</strong>: Looping through a list to find matches.</li>
            <li><strong>Sorting</strong>: Ordering items. Python's `sort()` or `sorted()` is stable and efficient (Timsort).</li>
            <li><strong>File I/O</strong>: Reading lines, stripping whitespace, splitting by `|`.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <div class="info-content">
            <p><strong>1. Parsing Errors</strong></p>
            <p>When splitting `line.split("|")`, if the file has empty lines, it will crash. Use `if not line: continue`.</p>
            
            <p><strong>2. Lambda Functions</strong></p>
            <p>For `sort_logs`, using `key=lambda x: x[key]` is Pythonic but confusing for beginners. They implement a custom sort (Bubble Sort) or use helper functions.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Solution Code</h2>
        <div class="code-block" style="background: #1e1e1e; color: #d4d4d4; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto;">
            <pre><code>def load_logs_from_file(filepath):
    logs = []
    with open(filepath, "r") as f:
        for line in f:
            parts = line.strip().split("|")
            if len(parts) == 4:
                logs.append({
                    "timestamp": parts[0],
                    "author": parts[1],
                    "content": parts[2],
                    "corrupted": parts[3] == "True"
                })
    return logs

def search_logs(logs, author):
    # Linear Search
    results = []
    for log in logs:
        if log["author"] == author:
            results.append(log)
    return results

def sort_logs(logs, key, descending=False):
    # Python Timsort
    return sorted(logs, key=lambda x: x[key], reverse=descending)</code></pre>
